{"name":"Nevermind Nirvana","hex":"#7bc8f6","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"nevermind","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Attention, heed.","Concern, affair."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To ignore.","Alternative spelling of never mind."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nevermind"},{"term":"nirvana","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Complete cessation of dukkha; a blissful state attained through realization of sunyata; enlightened experience.","State of paradise; heightened or great pleasure or peace; a state of universal perfection and bliss."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nirvana"}],"entries":[{"title":"Nevermind","blocks":[{"text":"Nevermind is the second studio album by American rock band Nirvana, released on September 24, 1991, by DGC Records. Produced by Butch Vig, Nevermind features a more polished, radio-friendly sound than the band's prior work. It was recorded at Sound City Studios in Van Nuys, California, and Smart Studios in Madison, Wisconsin, in May and June 1991, and mastered that August at the Mastering Lab in Hollywood, California. Nevermind was Nirvana's first album to feature drummer Dave Grohl."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/b7/NirvanaNevermindalbumcover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nevermind","wikidata":"Q17444"},{"title":"Nirvana","blocks":[{"text":"Nirvana, in the Indian religions, is the concept of an individual's passions being extinguished as the ultimate state of salvation, release, or liberation from suffering (duḥkha) and from the cycle of birth and rebirth (saṃsāra)."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nirvana","wikidata":"Q42927"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Butch_Vig_at_Smart_Studios%2C_Madison%2C_WI%2C_United_States_of_America.jpg/500px-Butch_Vig_at_Smart_Studios%2C_Madison%2C_W